Well, the part I removed was presumably intended to make it stop once it reached the target tile. It's a strange way to do it, though. If it works, it probably only works in Set Movement Route, not in a route set in the left panel of the event. Set Movement Route sets a forced movement route for the event; this code seems to
replace
the forced movement route with a blank one, which will expire immediately. But once it expires, it'll go back to whatever its regular movement route is; so it'll probably have no effect if used in an event's default movement route.
So… were you using that code in Set Movement Route? Or, did you put it in the event's default movement route?
If you need something that works in the event's default movement route, I think the best option would be to set a self switch that activates a different page. If that's not an option, you could rewrite the movement route settings. This is something that might be reset when the map is reloaded, though. At minimum, that happens when you leave the map and re-enter, but some things of this nature also reset by simply opening and closing the menu. I'm not sure if this would.
Self switch method (replace A with a different self switch if needed):
JavaScript:
var d = this.findDirectionTo(9, 9);
if(!d) {
    $gameSelfSwitches.setValue([$gameMap.mapId(), this.eventId(), 'A'], true);
    this.setMovementSuccess(true);
} else this.moveStraight(d);
Rewriting movement route settings method:
JavaScript:
var d = this.findDirectionTo(9, 9);
if(!d) {
    this._moveRoute.repeat = false;
    this.setMovementSuccess(true);
} else this.moveStraight(d);
If you
were
using a Set Movement Route all along, then I probably need more information. For example, if one enemy's destination being occupied happens to block the other enemy's only route to its own destination, then the second enemy would certainly stop once the first enemy reaches its destination.
